# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repo is public. Nothing in these files may ever be committed:
# the VAPID private key, the Supabase service-role key, the Paddle
# webhook secret, the Turnstile secret, the Google client secret.
# They belong only in Supabase → Edge Functions → Secrets.
.env
.env.*
*.secret
*secrets*
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.branches/

# ── Not for a public repo ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The original workbook. It is the source this app came from and it
# contains a real weekly schedule — which is personal information, and
# the exact category of data this app promises to keep private. Keep
# your copy locally; publishing it would be an odd first act for a
# privacy policy that says schedules are yours.
Countdown Timers.xlsx

# Local Claude Code tool permissions. Harmless, but it is machine
# config, and it spells out the absolute path of your Drive folder.
.claude/

# ── Working files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.bak
*.tmp
~$*
.~lock.*#

# ── Windows / Google Drive noise ──────────────────────────────────
desktop.ini
Thumbs.db

# ── .NET build output ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The published .scr is 150+MB (a self-contained runtime bundle) and
# is a build artifact, not source -- same reasoning as node_modules/
# in a JS repo. `dotnet publish` regenerates it from source in seconds.
windows-screensaver/bin/
windows-screensaver/obj/
